- Precedence tests (nested over flat) cover REQ-PATTERN-CONFIG and REQ-EXAMPLE-MESSAGES explicitly.

- Jest run (npm test -- --runInBand --verbose) shows tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts passing as part of the full suite:
  - Suite: "Valid Annotation Format Rule (Story 005.0-DEV-ANNOTATION-VALIDATION)"
  - All cases including [REQ-CONFIGURABLE-PATTERNS-*], [REQ-PATTERN-CONFIG], [REQ-EXAMPLE-MESSAGES], and [REQ-CONFIGURABLE-PATTERNS-FALLBACK*] passed.

6) Integration consistency with valid-story-reference
- valid-annotation-format’s default storyPattern `/^docs\/stories\/[0-9]+\.[0-9]+-DEV-[\w-]+\.story\.md$/` matches the default extension and primary directory used by src/rules/valid-story-reference.ts (defaultStoryDirs = ["docs/stories", "stories"], requireStoryExtension = true), satisfying REQ-CONSISTENCY and the "Integration" acceptance criterion that the configurable format validation works alongside valid-story-reference.

7) Documentation for custom pattern configuration
- docs/rules/valid-annotation-format.md documents nested and flat configuration, the precedence rules, and invalid configuration behavior. It explicitly mentions this story:
  ```md
  @story docs/stories/010.1-DEV-CONFIGURABLE-PATTERNS.story.md
  ...
  - `storyPathPattern`, `storyPathExample`, `requirementIdPattern`, `requirementIdExample` flat fields map directly to nested `story`/`req`.
  - When both nested and flat fields are provided, nested takes precedence.
  - If any pattern fields contain an invalid regular expression source … the rule reports `invalidRuleConfiguration` and falls back to built-in defaults.
  ```
- user-docs/api-reference.md (user-facing) includes the same options and explains nested vs flat usage, with descriptions of `storyPathPattern`, `requirementIdPattern`, and the corresponding example fields, fulfilling the Documentation acceptance criterion.

8) Coverage and quality
- coverage/lcov-report/src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-options.ts.html shows 99.32% statements and 97.87% branches covered, including the new configuration logic. This confirms the configurable pattern handling is thoroughly exercised by tests.

All acceptance criteria from docs/stories/010.1-DEV-CONFIGURABLE-PATTERNS.story.md are implemented and covered:
- Core Functionality: `storyPathPattern` / `requirementIdPattern` (and nested equivalents) are implemented and used by valid-annotation-format.
- Backward Compatibility: Defaults and fallback behavior ensure prior behavior when options are absent or invalid.
- Pattern Validation: Regex patterns are compiled with try/catch, configuration errors accumulated and reported without breaking rule execution.
- Error Messages: `storyPathExample` / `requirementIdExample` (and nested examples) feed into detailed error messages via buildStoryErrorMessage/buildReqErrorMessage.
- Quality Standards: Options are validated by JSON Schema with additionalProperties: false and thoroughly tested.
- Integration: Defaults align with valid-story-reference’s defaults and both rules can be configured consistently.
- Documentation: Rule docs and API reference describe configuration options and give concrete examples matching the story’s use cases.
